    "interviewAnswers": [
      {
        "question": "RAG vs fine-tuning: when would you pick each, and when would you combine both?",
        "answer": "Pick RAG when your data changes frequently (inventory, company rulebooks, news), when you need verifiable document citations, when users have different permission levels, or when zero-hallucination factual precision is mandatory. Pick Fine-Tuning when you need to teach a model a specialized tone, domain taxonomy, or strict structured output schemas (JSON/YAML), or when you want to reduce prompt latency by baking knowledge into weights. Combine both in production: Fine-tune a lightweight model (e.g. LLaMA 3 8B) on your domain syntax, output structure, and citation extraction format, and use RAG at runtime to feed it fresh, verifiable enterprise documents."
      },
      {
        "question": "An organization has thousands of documents with different access permissions. How would you design a RAG system so users can only retrieve documents they’re authorized to see?",
        "answer": "We implement Single-Stage Metadata Filtering tied to user JWT identity claims: 1) During ingestion, every document chunk is tagged with its parent document's Access Control List (ACL) metadata (e.g. allowed_groups, clearance_level, tenant_id). 2) At query time, the API Gateway validates the user's JWT and extracts their security group claims. 3) The vector database applies a single-stage pre-filter ($and: [{allowed_groups: {$in: user_groups}}]) directly during index graph traversal. We never post-filter, because discarding unauthorized chunks after retrieval causes severe recall collapse."
      },
      {
        "question": "Your chatbot takes 10 seconds to respond because you send the entire 50-page manual with every prompt. It's too slow and expensive. How would you fix it?",
        "answer": "Re-sending a 50-page manual with every prompt is paying to re-read the same book for every question. I fix this with a 6-step architectural pipeline: 1) Retrieve instead of resending: Chunk and embed the manual once, retrieving only the top 3-5 relevant chunks (~600 tokens vs 30,000 tokens). 2) Chunk with structure: Split on headings, keep titles/page numbers as metadata, and overlap lightly so steps aren't cut in half. 3) Rerank, then trim: Use a cross-encoder to reorder candidates and keep only what clears a threshold. 4) Cache what repeats: Use prompt caching for static instructions and semantic Redis caching for frequent questions. 5) Control output: Stream the response and cap max_tokens. 6) Measure: Track tokens per request, TTFT, and answer accuracy. The principle: send the model the few paragraphs that answer the question, not the whole document."
      }
    ],
